id: k8s.node
kind: kubernetes
title: Node
aliases: node, worker node
tags: architecture, capacity
questions:
  - what is on a node and how much can it run?
invariant: A node is a machine (or VM) with a kubelet, a container runtime and allocatable capacity that the scheduler fills with Pods.
answer_30s: A node runs the kubelet, the container runtime and the Pods assigned to it; its allocatable capacity minus the requests already placed is what the scheduler can fill.
answer_2m: |
  Capacity, allocatable and allocated are three different numbers: system
  daemons reserve part of capacity, so allocatable is smaller, and the sum of
  running Pod requests is allocated. Node conditions (Ready, MemoryPressure,
  DiskPressure, PIDPressure) drive eviction of the lowest-priority Pods when a
  resource is exhausted. This is where requests stop being bookkeeping and
  become the scheduling currency, and where taints and tolerations express
  "which workloads may live here".
when_to_use:
  - capacity planning, Pending-Pod diagnosis, node pressure and eviction reasoning
commands:
  - kubectl get nodes -o wide => readiness, roles, kubelet version, node IPs
  - kubectl describe node <node> => capacity, allocated requests, taints, conditions
  - kubectl top node => current usage against allocatable capacity
failure_modes:
  - a node in MemoryPressure evicting Pods that had no requests set
  - cordoned node forgotten, so capacity is silently missing
related:
  - k8s.scheduler
  - k8s.kubelet
  - k8s.resources.requests
  - k8s.daemon-set
sources:
  - https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/architecture/nodes/
